<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Moreover,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="g_{\eta}(y)=\nabla V(x_{y}^{+})+\eta^{-1/2}\,\mathbb{E}_{\mathsf{r}_{y}}\nabla\rho_{y}(U)\,,\qquad\widehat{g}_{\eta}(y;G)=\nabla V(x_{y}^{+})+\eta^{-1/2}\,\nabla\rho_{y}(G)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>𝗋</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>η</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\eta}(y)=\nabla V(x_{y}^{+})+\eta^{-1/2}\,\mathbb{E}_{\mathsf{r}_{y}}\nabla\rho_{y}(U)\,,\qquad\widehat{g}_{\eta}(y;G)=\nabla V(x_{y}^{+})+\eta^{-1/2}\,\nabla\rho_{y}(G)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">The gradient of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz, so
(<a href="#S4.E6" title="Equation 4.6 ‣ Proof. ‣ 4.1 Setup and smoothing estimates ‣ 4 Proof of the 𝑊_2 guarantee ‣ Smoothed Picard Hamiltonian Monte Carlo" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>) gives (<a href="#S4.E2" title="Equation 4.2 ‣ Lemma 4.2 (Stochastic gradient estimates). ‣ 4.1 Setup and smoothing estimates ‣ 4 Proof of the 𝑊_2 guarantee ‣ Smoothed Picard Hamiltonian Monte Carlo" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>). Since <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{g}_{\eta}(y;\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>η</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{g}_{\eta}(y;\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz, the standard Gaussian concentration inequality
gives (<a href="#S4.E3" title="Equation 4.3 ‣ Lemma 4.2 (Stochastic gradient estimates). ‣ 4.1 Setup and smoothing estimates ‣ 4 Proof of the 𝑊_2 guarantee ‣ Smoothed Picard Hamiltonian Monte Carlo" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>), and the same observation gives
(<a href="#S4.E5" title="Equation 4.5 ‣ Lemma 4.2 (Stochastic gradient estimates). ‣ 4.1 Setup and smoothing estimates ‣ 4 Proof of the 𝑊_2 guarantee ‣ Smoothed Picard Hamiltonian Monte Carlo" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>). Finally, <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\mapsto x_{y}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\mapsto x_{y}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> is non-expansive and
<math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla V</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz, proving (<a href="#S4.E4" title="Equation 4.4 ‣ Lemma 4.2 (Stochastic gradient estimates). ‣ 4.1 Setup and smoothing estimates ‣ 4 Proof of the 𝑊_2 guarantee ‣ Smoothed Picard Hamiltonian Monte Carlo" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>